Add REALM_LIGHT_FIX (ON BY DEFAULT) at the FORCE_LIGHT_INTENSITY definition

    Half one of two. The companion hunk is light_normalize_dataset_object_og391.patch;
    the two files MUST be applied together -- see the runtime assert there. Applying only
    this half gives intensity 150000 WITH normalize=True, which is ~35x too bright.

    ROOT CAUSE. Two OmniGibson changes between 1.1.1 and 3.9.1 cancel each other and
    leave a PER-SCENE lighting error:

        macros.py                 1.1.1: FORCE_LIGHT_INTENSITY = 150000   3.9.1: = 10000
        objects/dataset_object.py 1.1.1: sets inputs:intensity only       3.9.1: ALSO
                                                                         sets normalize=True

    `normalize=True` divides emission by the light's AREA, so the two cancel EXACTLY at
    area = 1/15 = 0.0667 m^2. Scenes whose lights sit near that area already match 1.1.1;
    scenes with smaller lights are too bright. The defect scales with light SIZE, not
    light count -- measured light counts run 10 to 108 across the ten task scenes while
    the flag-on ratios all land inside a 0.28 band. `normalize=True` is authored on 107
    of 108 og391 lights and on none of the 108 og111 lights.

    WHAT THE FLAG BUYS. Over the 7 comparable tasks on the exterior cam1, per-task ratio
    spread against the 1.1.1 references collapses from 0.596 (x0.968-x1.564) to 0.276
    (x1.056-x1.331) -- 2.2x tighter, mean x1.146 -> x1.180. It does NOT make og391 match
    1.1.1: a uniform ~15-35% excess remains, belonging to tonemapping/exposure and the
    material re-export. The point is that the residual becomes UNIFORM, which one global
    exposure term could absorb and a per-scene shift never could.

    The trade is not uniformly favourable per task: the flag fixes rotate_mug
    (x1.564 -> x1.188) and mildly worsens the three tasks that already matched (0, 1, 7
    go from x1.01-x1.03 to x1.15-x1.22). Judge it on the spread, not on any single task,
    and do NOT stack an appearance-tuned intensity scale on top -- per task that looks
    like an improvement while destroying the only property the flag provides.

    DEFAULT. On, as of 2026-08-18, after review of the rendered off/on comparison across
    all ten tasks. REALM_LIGHT_FIX=0 still selects stock OG 3.9.1 lighting and still beats
    the default; that path is bit-identical to a fully unpatched container (cam1 184.510 vs
    184.508, and the light-prim readback shows 107x intensity=10000 normalize=True either
    way). Numbers from job 192356; table at logs/lightfix_10task/lightfix_table.txt.

--- a/omnigibson/macros.py
+++ b/omnigibson/macros.py
@@ -203,6 +203,64 @@
 # Whether to enable transition rules or not
 gm.ENABLE_TRANSITION_RULES = True
 
+# Whether to run update_visuals() on stateful objects each step. Visual state (Frozen, Burnt,
+# Cooked, OnFire, ...) only affects textures/emitters, so this is pure overhead when running
+# headless without RGB observations.
+gm.ENABLE_VISUAL_UPDATES = True
+
+# Whitelist of object state class names to update each step. If None, all states that require
+# per-step updates are updated. Set to a list of class names (e.g. ["ToggledOn"]) to skip
+# expensive irrelevant state updates. Note that this only filters the per-step update loop --
+# every state type is still globally initialized, so on-demand queries keep working.
+gm.OBJECT_STATE_UPDATE_WHITELIST = None
+
+# Restricts which rigid links get PhysxContactReportAPI applied at load time. None (default) keeps
+# upstream behavior: every non-visual-only rigid link reports contacts. Otherwise a list of regex
+# patterns matched against each link's prim path -- only matching links report contacts.
+#
+# This shrinks the RigidContactAPI contact matrix at the source: get_body_filters() keys off the same
+# flag, so both the PhysX-side contact reporting and the O(R*C) Python bookkeeping shrink together.
+#
+# WARNING: links excluded here are invisible to every contact-based query -- Touching, ContactBodies,
+# AttachedTo, assisted grasping, particle modifiers and any transition rule that depends on them.
+# Only set this when you know exactly which bodies your task interrogates. Read at object load time,
+# so it must be set before the scene is loaded.
+#
+# Vector envs: prim paths are /World/scene_<idx>/<object>/<link>, so patterns must NOT be anchored on
+# a specific scene index or only that one scene reports contacts. Match on object/link names instead.
+gm.CONTACT_REPORTING_PATTERNS = None
+
+# Restricts per-step work to objects near a robot. Objects whose AABB is farther than
+# PROXIMITY_GATE_RADIUS metres from every robot's AABB are dropped from the RigidContactAPI contact
+# matrix (both rows and columns) and skipped by the per-step object state loop. They still render,
+# still collide, and remain fully queryable on demand -- only their per-step bookkeeping is skipped.
+#
+# Safe for a fixed-base manipulator: a body beyond the robot's reach cannot be in contact with it, so
+# removing it from the contact matrix cannot change any contact answer involving the robot.
+#
+# TURN THIS OFF FOR MOBILE MANIPULATION. The gate is evaluated when the contact view is built (on
+# play, and whenever object handles are rebuilt), not every step, so a base that drives across the
+# room would keep the membership it had at initialization.
+gm.PROXIMITY_GATE_ENABLED = True
+
+# Radius in metres, measured between object AABBs rather than origins, so a long counter counts as
+# near if any part of it is within reach. Must comfortably exceed the robot's workspace: anything
+# outside is frozen out of per-step state updates, so a task object beyond it would stop updating.
+gm.PROXIMITY_GATE_RADIUS = 1.5
+
+# Whether RigidContactAPI folds each physics step into running (R, C) accumulators as it arrives,
+# instead of cloning every step and reducing the whole batch in update_contact_cache(). Measured on a
+# REALM kitchen rollout, update_contact_cache is ~97% of the entire non-physics step, and the batched
+# reduction is where it goes: 3 clones per substep, then a stack plus several (N, R, C) intermediates
+# including an (N, R, C) int64. Folding produces identical contact matrices (see
+# tests/test_contact_cache_equivalence.py) while never materializing anything larger than (R, C).
+gm.INCREMENTAL_CONTACT_CACHE = False
+
+# Whether sim.step() renders by default. When False, sim.step() only runs physics and you must
+# call og.sim.render() explicitly to refresh camera/sensor data. Object states are still updated;
+# use sim.step(blind=True) / env.step_blind() to skip those as well.
+gm.RENDER_ON_STEP = True
+
 # Default settings for the omni UI viewer
 gm.DEFAULT_VIEWER_WIDTH = 1280
 gm.DEFAULT_VIEWER_HEIGHT = 720
@@ -214,7 +272,72 @@
 gm.DEFAULT_PHYSICS_FREQ = 120
 
 # Forced light intensity for all DatasetObjects. None if the USD-provided intensities should be respected.
-gm.FORCE_LIGHT_INTENSITY = 10000
+#
+# REALM_LIGHT_FIX -- ON BY DEFAULT as of 2026-08-18. Set REALM_LIGHT_FIX=0 to get stock OG 3.9.1
+# behaviour back, which is bit-identical to every run made before this knob existed (verified: cam1
+# 184.510 with the flag off against an unpatched container's 184.508, and the light-prim readback
+# shows 107x intensity=10000 normalize=True either way).
+#
+# It defaults ON because Martin reviewed the rendered off/on comparison and adopted the flag-on look
+# for every task. It shipped opt-in for one day first; the default flip is the only thing that
+# changed, and an explicit REALM_LIGHT_FIX=0 still wins, so recorded comparisons stay reproducible.
+#
+# WHY THIS EXISTS. Two OmniGibson changes between 1.1.1 and 3.9.1 cancel each other and leave a
+# PER-SCENE lighting error:
+#
+#     macros.py                 1.1.1: FORCE_LIGHT_INTENSITY = 150000   3.9.1: = 10000  (15x lower)
+#     objects/dataset_object.py 1.1.1: sets inputs:intensity only       3.9.1: ALSO sets
+#                                                                              inputs:normalize=True
+#
+# `normalize=True` divides emission by the light's AREA, so the two changes cancel EXACTLY at
+# area = 1/15 = 0.0667 m^2. Scenes whose lights sit near that area already match 1.1.1
+# (push_switch x1.03, put_green_block x1.01); scenes with smaller lights are too bright
+# (rotate_mug's rect lights are 0.028 m^2 -> x1.56 on the exterior cam). The defect scales with
+# light SIZE, not light count. `normalize=True` is authored on 107 of 108 og391 lights and on none
+# of the 108 og111 lights.
+#
+# Setting this to 1 restores 1.1.1's exact light configuration: intensity 150000, and NO normalize
+# write. That does NOT make og391 match 1.1.1 -- a uniform ~15-35% excess remains, which belongs to
+# tonemapping/exposure and to the material re-export, not to lights. The point is that it makes the
+# error UNIFORM: over the 7 comparable tasks on the exterior cam1, per-task ratio spread collapses
+# from 0.596 (x0.968-x1.564) to 0.276 (x1.056-x1.331) -- 2.2x tighter, mean x1.146 -> x1.180 --
+# converting a per-scene domain shift that no downstream correction can fix into one global gain a
+# single exposure term could absorb.
+#
+# NUMBERS CORRECTED 2026-08-18. This comment previously claimed 0.53 -> 0.11, "5x tighter", from
+# x1.199/x1.203/x1.313. Those were three tasks measured off ladder rungs, which carry a ~+10 luma
+# under-settle bias. The dedicated 18-boot sweep (job 192356, all ten tasks, off and on, 300
+# pre-renders and a 5-frame median each) supersedes them, and 2.2x is what the full task set gives.
+# A mid-sweep read of the first three tasks briefly suggested 14x -- also three tasks, same error of
+# generalising from the tasks that happen to agree. Table:
+# ~/projects/REALM/logs/lightfix_10task/lightfix_table.txt
+#
+# The trade is NOT uniformly favourable per task, and that is expected: the flag fixes rotate_mug
+# (x1.564 -> x1.188) and mildly WORSENS the three tasks that already matched (0, 1, 7 go from
+# x1.01-x1.03 to x1.15-x1.22). Judge it on the spread, not on any single task.
+#
+# DO NOT pair this with an appearance-tuned intensity scale. That destroys exactly the property
+# above: on push_switch, which already matches within 3%, a x3 scale moves the mean from +3.4% to
+# -15.0% and degrades the affine fit (slope 0.782 -> 0.610, R2 0.72 -> 0.51).
+#
+# The env var is read HERE, once, at import time, so a run's setting is visible in its environment
+# and the resolved state is logged below. objects/dataset_object.py consumes gm.REALM_LIGHT_FIX
+# rather than re-reading the environment, so the intensity and the normalize write can never
+# disagree -- intensity 150000 WITH normalize=True would be ~35x too bright.
+_REALM_LIGHT_FIX_RAW = os.environ.get("REALM_LIGHT_FIX")
+gm.REALM_LIGHT_FIX = (_REALM_LIGHT_FIX_RAW or "1").strip().lower() in ("1", "true", "yes", "on")
+gm.FORCE_LIGHT_INTENSITY = 150000 if gm.REALM_LIGHT_FIX else 10000
+
+# Log the resolved state UNCONDITIONALLY: no run may be ambiguous about which lighting it used.
+# macros.py is imported before OmniGibson configures logging, so this is a print, not a logger call.
+print(
+    "[REALM_LIGHT_FIX] {} -- FORCE_LIGHT_INTENSITY={} normalize_write={} (env REALM_LIGHT_FIX={})".format(
+        "ON (OG 1.1.1 lighting)" if gm.REALM_LIGHT_FIX else "off (stock OG 3.9.1 lighting)",
+        gm.FORCE_LIGHT_INTENSITY,
+        "skipped" if gm.REALM_LIGHT_FIX else "True",
+        "unset" if _REALM_LIGHT_FIX_RAW is None else repr(_REALM_LIGHT_FIX_RAW),
+    )
+)
 
 # Forced roughness for all DatasetObjects. None if the USD-provided roughness maps should be respected.
 gm.FORCE_ROUGHNESS = 0.7
